  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 255 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An unsettled pattern develops later today into the \nweekend, with daily chances for showers or thunderstorms. \n\nSurface high pressure is now well offshore in the Atlantic with an \nupper trough and surface low across Quebec. The associated cold \nfront extends from the low southwestward into the Ohio Valley. An \nupper ridge is noted across the central and eastern Gulf Coast, \nextending northeast into the local area. \n\nThe northern upper trough and associated surface low will slowly \nshift east-southeast today, gradually suppressing the ridge. The \ncold front approaches from the northwest this evening, slowing as \nthe boundary aligns parallel to the mid-and upper-level quasi-zonal \nflow. Compressional heating ahead of the front will yield highs \nreaching the low-to-mid 90s inland from the immediate coast. Latest \nguidance has maintained the potential for isolated to widely \nscattered convection entering western areas late this afternoon, \nspreading across the Virginia Northern Neck and Maryland Lower \nEastern Shore tonight into early Friday. While most of the stronger \nmid level flow will remain displaced north of the local area, 20-25 \nkt of deep layer shear will be in place late this afternoon into the \nevening hours. Combined with MLCAPE of 1000-1500 J/kg, a few \nstronger thunderstorm cores are possible across the northern half of \nthe area with the potential for locally strong straight line winds. \nSPC has introduced a Marginal Risk (lvl 1/5) for strong to severe \nstorms this afternoon and tonight, primarily for the northern half \nof the CWA.\n\n00z models continue to slow the progression of the front across the \nregion Friday. The 00z HREF shows a signal for locally heavy \nrainfall in areas that remain along and south of the stalled front, \nprimarily NE portions of NC and along the VA/NC border counties \nfarther west. High temps will vary considerably with low 80s N and \nlow 90s S. \n\nBetter areawide storm chances arrive Saturday as the stalled front \nlifts back north and a few weak perturbations push across the \nregion. Considerable cloud cover and scattered to numerous showers \nand storms, including the potential for some locally heavy \ndownpours, will cap Saturday's highs in the low to mid 80s for most, \nwith upper 80s limited to the southern third of the CWA. There \nremains an area wide ERO that would suggest some potential hydro \nissues in the typical spots for Saturday, given the moist, modestly \nunstable airmass and relatively weak steering flow.\n\nThe front washes out late weekend into early next week. Some \nadditional showers and storms remain possible Sunday, though \ncoverage will be noticeably lower than Saturday.\n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Drier next week as an upper ridge builds into the \nregion with above normal temperatures.\n\nMostly sunny and dry conditions are expected on Monday with only a \nslight chance for a shower or two across NE NC in the late afternoon \nor early evening hours. Temperatures will be in the mid to upper 80s \nwith a few spots across the south possibly touching 90 degrees. \nContinued dry Tuesday with upper 80s and low 90s in the forecast. \nChances for showers and storms tick up mid to late week with \ntemperatures continuing above seasonal norms as upper ridging across \nthe Gulf Coast builds back toward the region.",
